      <h1>The evolution of HTTP and HTTPS</h1>
      <p>From simple document requests to encrypted, multiplexed connections. Explore how the Web’s conversation evolved.</p>
    </header>
    
      <section aria-labelledby="overview">
        <h2 id="overview">A small protocol. A changing Web.</h2>
        <p>Every time a browser asks for a page, a conversation begins. HTTP defines the requests and responses in that conversation. Its evolution shows how the Web adapted from linked documents to applications, APIs, and media.</p>
        <p>This research follows HTTP from its early form through HTTP/3, then explains how HTTPS protects the connection. The central idea is continuity: familiar methods and status codes remain useful even when message formats and transports change.</p>
        <p class="source-note">Research basis: <a href="references.html#rfc9110">RFC 9110</a>, <a href="references.html#rfc9113">RFC 9113</a>, and <a href="references.html#rfc9114">RFC 9114</a>.</p>
